# JBA9 Mac Toolkit

An all-in-one, interactive macOS admin script for removing/reinstalling Microsoft 365,
cleaning up leftover sign-in data, and installing a set of common apps. Not tied to Jamf —
run manually from Terminal on any Mac.

## Quick start

```bash
curl -o jba9-mac-toolkit.sh https://raw.githubusercontent.com/jba9gla/GCU-Scirpts/refs/heads/main/office365/jba9-mac-toolkit.sh
chmod +x jba9-mac-toolkit.sh
sudo ./jba9-mac-toolkit.sh
```

Must be run with `sudo` — removal and installs touch `/Applications` and `/Library`.

If `curl` reports "command not found" after downloading, the executable bit likely
didn't survive the download — re-run `chmod +x jba9-mac-toolkit.sh` and try again.
If macOS blocks it as downloaded-from-the-internet, clear the quarantine flag:

```bash
xattr -d com.apple.quarantine jba9-mac-toolkit.sh 2>/dev/null
```

## What it does

On launch you get a menu with four categories:

### 1. Removal
- Remove Microsoft 365 license + uninstall Office (apps, support files, containers)
- Full clean — license removal + uninstall + login/auth remnant cleanup in one pass
- Remove license only, keep apps installed (useful for "wrong account signed in")

### 2. Login / Auth
- Report-only check for leftover Office sign-in data (keychain, identity broker
  cache, preference domains, login items) — no changes made
- Clean version of the same check, with Microsoft Teams handled separately via an
  explicit Keep/Remove prompt (Teams is often used standalone, independent of Office)
- Verify removal status — confirms apps, license files, and activation keychain
  entries are actually gone before you reinstall
- Clear Safari's Microsoft/Office web session data — stops Outlook/Office silently
  auto-signing in via a lingering Safari session instead of prompting for credentials

### 3. Install / Reinstall
Cached downloads from official sources, installed silently where possible:

| App | Source | Method |
|---|---|---|
| Microsoft 365 (full suite) | Microsoft CDN | Direct `.pkg`, silent install |
| Microsoft Teams (standalone) | Microsoft CDN | Direct `.pkg`, silent install |
| Google Chrome | Google | Direct `.pkg`, silent install |
| Brave Browser | Brave | Direct `.dmg`, app copied to `/Applications` |
| Google Drive | Google | Direct `.dmg` containing `.pkg`, silent install |
| Visual Studio Code | Microsoft | Direct `.zip`, extracted to `/Applications` |
| Homebrew | Homebrew | Official bootstrap install script |
| Cyberduck | Homebrew cask, or browser fallback | No stable direct-download URL exists |
| The Unarchiver | Homebrew cask, or browser fallback | No stable direct-download URL exists |
| AppCleaner | Homebrew cask, or browser fallback | No stable direct-download URL exists |
| Adobe Acrobat Reader | Homebrew cask, or browser fallback | No stable direct-download URL exists |
| Bitdefender | Opens bitdefender.com | Account/tenant-specific installer — can't be hardcoded |

Downloads use a browser-style User-Agent and a minimum file-size check to catch
cases where a vendor silently serves an error/redirect page instead of the real
installer (this was a real bug during development — see Known issues below).

### 4. Maintenance
- View cached installers and their location
- Clear the installer cache

## Where things are stored

- **Logs:** `/Library/Logs/JBA9Toolkit/jba9-toolkit-<timestamp>.log` — one log
  file per run, everything printed to the terminal is also written here
- **Cached installers:** `/Library/Application Support/JBA9ToolkitCache/` —
  persists across reboots and script runs, so re-running an install doesn't
  re-download multi-GB files every time. You'll be asked whether to reuse the
  cached copy or fetch a fresh one each time you install something.

## Notes and known issues

- **SIP-protected container folders:** After uninstalling Office, a few empty
  `~/Library/Containers/com.microsoft.*` folders may be left behind. These are
  inert (no app attached) and protected by macOS's sandboxing system even from
  root — this is expected macOS behaviour, not a script bug. Safe to ignore.
- **Safari session sign-in:** If Outlook/Office silently signs in without
  prompting, it's usually because Safari holds an active Microsoft/Office web
  session (via `ASWebAuthenticationSession`), not a leftover file. Use
  Login/Auth → "Clear Safari Microsoft/Office session" to fix this.
- **Some Mac usernames trigger auto-suggested accounts:** If your local macOS
  short username matches the local part of a GCU email address, some Microsoft
  apps will auto-suggest that account purely from a username/domain guess, with
  zero stored credentials anywhere. This is expected Microsoft app behaviour,
  not something this script controls.
- **Cyberduck / The Unarchiver / AppCleaner / Adobe Acrobat Reader** have no
  stable "latest" direct-download URL (versioned filenames only), so these
  install via Homebrew if it's present, falling back to opening the official
  download page in the browser otherwise.
- **Bitdefender** is intentionally never auto-downloaded — GravityZone/business
  and consumer installers are both account-specific, tied to your organisation's
  console. The script just opens bitdefender.com for you to grab the correct one.

## Requirements

- macOS with `sudo` access
- Network access to: `raw.githubusercontent.com`, `go.microsoft.com`,
  `dl.google.com`, `brave-browser-downloads.s3.brave.com`,
  `code.visualstudio.com`, and (if using Homebrew-based installs)
  `github.com` / `raw.githubusercontent.com` for the Homebrew installer itself

